3. Copper Cable ⭐

Copper cables transmit data using electrical signals.

Common Ethernet copper cables include:

Cat5e
Cat6
Cat6a
Cat8
Advantages
Relatively inexpensive
Easy to install
Commonly used for LAN connections
Supports Power over Ethernet (PoE) in appropriate implementations
Disadvantages
Limited distance compared with fiber
More susceptible to electromagnetic interference
Performance depends on cable category and installation quality
Real-World Use
PC → Ethernet Cable → Switch
IP Phone → Ethernet Cable → Switch
Access Point → Ethernet Cable → Switch
4. Fiber Optic ⭐

Fiber optic cable transmits data using light signals through optical fiber.

It is commonly used where high bandwidth and long-distance communication are required.

Types

Single-mode fiber (SMF)

Designed for longer distances
Common in telecom and long-distance infrastructure

Multimode fiber (MMF)

Generally used for shorter distances
Common in some data-center and building environments
Advantages
Very high bandwidth
Long-distance transmission
Low electromagnetic interference
Useful for high-speed network connections
Disadvantages
More expensive than basic copper
Installation and repair can require specialized equipment
Fiber components can be more delicate
Real-World Use
Data centers
ISP networks
Telecom networks
Long-distance links
High-speed switch-to-switch connections
