6. Simplex, Half-Duplex & Full-Duplex ⭐

These terms describe the direction of communication between two devices.

Simplex

Communication happens in only one direction.

A ─────────→ B

B cannot send data back to A through that communication channel.

Example

Traditional examples include:

Broadcast television
Radio broadcasting
Half-Duplex

Communication can happen in both directions, but not simultaneously.

A ─────→ B

A ←───── B

One side communicates at a time.

Example
Walkie-talkie

When one person speaks, the other waits.

Full-Duplex ⭐

Communication can happen in both directions simultaneously.

A ─────────→ B
A ←───────── B

Both sides can send and receive at the same time.

Example
Modern switched Ethernet
Telephone communication

Network media is the medium through which data travels between network devices. It can be wired, such as copper and fiber optic, or wireless, such as Wi-Fi. Copper uses electrical signals, fiber uses light, and wireless uses radio signals. Transmission can also be simplex, half-duplex, or full-duplex depending on the direction of communication.